\title{Calibration Equals Maximal Robustness:\\ The Exact Breakdown Number of a Quota Rung}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{17 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
An ensemble of $n$ stochastic replications of an experiment --- runs differing only in a random
seed --- produces $n$ numerical readings. Reporting a single number means choosing a \emph{rung}
of the ensemble's \emph{quota ladder}: for each quota $m\in\{1,\dots,n\}$, the least budget at
which at least $m$ of the runs succeed, i.e.\ the $m$-th order statistic. We develop the theory
of these rungs along two independent axes and prove that the axes coincide. On the probabilistic
axis, modelling each seed as clearing a fixed bar independently with probability $p$, the $m$-th
rung's distribution function is the binomial upper tail
$R_n(m,p)=\sum_{j\ge m}\binom{n}{j}p^{j}(1-p)^{n-j}$; we prove the \emph{parity law of
calibration}: $R_n(m,\tfrac12)=\tfrac12$ if and only if $2m=n+1$. Hence an ensemble admits a
calibrated rung iff $n$ is odd, and then uniquely; every even ensemble has none, with explicit
central defect $\delta_r=\binom{2r}{r}2^{-(2r+1)}$ satisfying
$\tfrac{1}{2\sqrt{4r+1}}\le\delta_r\le\tfrac{1}{2\sqrt{3r+1}}$ and
$\delta_r\sqrt{r}\to\tfrac{1}{2\sqrt{\pi}}$, whose two central rungs nevertheless average to
exactly $1/2$. On the combinatorial axis, a two-sided bracket with matching sharpness in both
directions yields the exact \emph{breakdown number} $\beta(n,m)=\min(m-1,\,n-m)$. The main
theorem, the \emph{Calibration--Robustness Dichotomy}, states that for $n=2r+1$ a rung is
calibrated iff $\beta(n,m)=r$; for even $n=2r$ both properties fail together. We apply the theory
to a measurement in which three seeds produced compression knees $\{160,224,256\}$ with median
$224=\tfrac78\cdot\tfrac{dL}{32}$ exactly, show that a fourth seed buys neither robustness nor
calibration while a fifth restores both, and quantify --- via a Condorcet analysis with an exactly
located crossing at $47$ seeds --- how far a three-seed centre is from certification.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords.} order statistics, binomial upper tail, breakdown point, robust
statistics, calibration, Condorcet jury theorem, central binomial coefficient, seed ensembles.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The reporting problem}

Empirical claims about stochastic systems are almost always claims about an ensemble. One runs an
experiment several times, changing nothing but a pseudo-random seed, and obtains several numbers;
the published claim is a single number. The map from ensemble to number --- worst case, best
case, mean, median, ``$k$ out of $n$'' --- is a \emph{reporting convention}, usually defended on
grounds of taste or tradition.

This paper argues that in the ordinal setting the convention is forced. We identify a canonical
family of readings (the rungs of the quota ladder), attach to each rung two independent quality
measures --- one probabilistic (calibration), one adversarial (breakdown number) --- and prove
that both are optimised by the same rung, and only by that rung. Since neither measure mentions
the other, the coincidence is a theorem worth isolating.

\subsection{The measurement}

The motivating data set concerns attention sparsification in autoregressive sequence models. For
width parameter $d$ and context length $L$, one asks for the least per-position retention budget
$k$ such that keeping only the top-$k$ attention weights preserves held-out accuracy above a fixed
fraction of the dense reference. Call that least $k$ the \emph{knee}.

At $(d,L)=(4,1024)$ three seeds gave knees $\{96,112,128\}$; at $(d,L)=(4,2048)$ three seeds gave
$\{256,224,160\}$. With $P=dL/32$ the natural product scale ($128$ and $256$ respectively), the two
knee sets are $\{0.75P,0.875P,1.0P\}$ and $\{0.625P,0.875P,1.0P\}$, with medians
$112=\tfrac78\cdot128$ and $224=\tfrac78\cdot256$ --- exactly $\tfrac78 P$ in both cases. Four point
predictions registered in advance for the third long-context seed ($224$, $240$, $256$, $192$) were
all refuted by the measured $160$, while the prediction about the \emph{median} held exactly. The
spread widened with context (from $0.25P$ to $0.375P$), entirely in the low tail; the upper edge
stayed pinned at $P$. Per-seed readings are noisy; the centre is stable. The theory below explains
why.

