seeds, repeated exactly.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Quota ladder.} Sort the knees ascending; $Q(m)$ is the $m$-th entry and $Q(0)=0$. Cost
$O(n\log n)$, or $O(n)$ by counting sort on a bounded budget grid. Rungs, gaps, windows and
contamination curves are then $O(1)$ look-ups.

\paragraph{Breakdown table and contamination curve.} $\beta(n,m)=\min(m-1,n-m)$ in $O(1)$; at level
$c\le\beta(n,m)$ the achievable interval is $[Q(m-c),Q(m+c)]$ with maximal bias
$\max\bigl(Q(m+c)-Q(m),\,Q(m)-Q(m-c)\bigr)$.

\paragraph{Rung tail and certification search.} Evaluate $R_n(m,p)$ by the term recurrence
$t_{j+1}=t_j\cdot\frac{(n-j)p}{(j+1)(1-p)}$, avoiding factorials, in $O(n-m)$ operations. To locate
the certification threshold at target miss level $\varepsilon$, scan $r=0,1,2,\dots$ until
$1-R_{2r+1}(r+1,p)<\varepsilon$; monotonicity (Theorem~\ref{thm:condorcet}) makes the first crossing
the answer, and Theorem~\ref{thm:rate} bounds the scan length by
$O\bigl(\log(1/\varepsilon)/\log\frac{1}{4p(1-p)}\bigr)$. Exact rational arithmetic turns the located
crossing into a certificate.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Relation to classical robust statistics}
The breakdown point of the sample median is classically $\lfloor (n-1)/2\rfloor/n\to1/2$ and that of
an extreme order statistic is $0$. The results above refine this into an exact per-rung count,
$\beta(n,m)=\min(m-1,n-m)$, in a setting where the reading is defined operationally. What is new is
not the value at the median but the \emph{equivalence} of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}: the same index is
selected by a calibration condition that never mentions corruption.

\subsection{Parity as the obstruction}
Theorem~\ref{thm:even} shows the even-ensemble failure is not cosmetic: the defect is of size
$\approx 1/(2\sqrt{\pi r})$, non-summable, and the robustness tie is simultaneous. Averaging the two
central rungs repairs calibration exactly but cannot break the robustness tie, since the averaged
reading inherits $\beta=r-1$.

\subsection{Practical guidance}
Report the median of an \emph{odd} ensemble. Treat published guarantees (max over seeds) as
maximally fragile, $\beta=0$. Increase ensembles by two, not one: $3\to4$ buys nothing on either
axis, $3\to5$ buys a strict robustness increment and restores calibration. Quote the contamination
curve $[Q(m-c),Q(m+c)]$ rather than a symmetric error bar; adversarial bias is generally asymmetric
(here $-64/+32$).

\subsection{Limitations}
The probability model treats seeds as exchangeable Bernoulli trials at a fixed budget; real seeds
may be dependent and their pass probability varies with the budget. The breakdown analysis is
worst-case and hence conservative for benign noise. The empirical median law rests on two contexts
and six seeds, with a $7/27$ miss probability under its own frequency model. Centre-minimality is
not verifiable at three seeds.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{The low-tail experiment.} A fourth seed at the long context is diagnostic for the
tail, not the centre: a fourth knee in $\{160,192\}$ would establish the $0.625P$ low tail as a
stable feature, while a value in $\{224,256\}$ would mark it seed-specific. The centre question
requires a fifth seed.
\item \textbf{Dependent seeds.} Replace independent Bernoulli seeds by an exchangeable model with
positive correlation and ask whether the parity law survives; the reflection identity is a symmetry
of the uniform measure on outcomes, so calibration plausibly persists for symmetric exchangeable
laws with a modified defect.
\item \textbf{Continuous budgets.} Extend the contamination curve to a continuous budget axis, where
window width becomes a quantile spread and centre-minimality becomes a log-concavity hypothesis on
the knee density.
\item \textbf{Beyond the median functional.} Characterise all reading functionals that are
simultaneously calibrated and of maximal breakdown; the even-ensemble averaging repair suggests
symmetric convex combinations of central rungs, with a strict robustness penalty for every
non-degenerate combination.
\item \textbf{Cost-optimal ensemble design.} With a per-seed cost and a target certification level,
the crossing analysis turns ensemble sizing into an integer program; the exact crossing at $47$
seeds for $p=2/3$, $\varepsilon=10^{-2}$ is the first entry of a table worth computing in general.
\item \textbf{The offset ladders.} For each fixed offset $k$ the off-centre ladder, started at its
smallest ensemble, sums to exactly $1-p^{2k+1}=(1-p)(1+p+\cdots+p^{2k})$; $k=0$ recovers $1-p$. The
combinatorial meaning of these generating functions for non-central reporting conventions deserves
a systematic treatment.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}

An ensemble of stochastic runs offers a ladder of readings, one per quota. Each rung carries a
probabilistic quality (does it lean on uninformative data?) and an adversarial quality (how many
corrupted runs does it survive?). The first is measured by the parity condition $2m=n+1$, the second
by the exact count $\min(m-1,n-m)$; for odd ensembles the two select the same rung and only that
rung, while for even ensembles both fail together. Applied to a measurement whose per-seed readings
were $\{160,224,256\}$, the theory explains why four sharp point predictions could all fail while
the prediction about the centre held --- the centre is the only functional of a three-seed ensemble
that is both calibrated and of positive breakdown number --- and it delivers the design verdict
that the next seed should be the fifth, not the fourth.

